#!|
  Discard patterns, and the exercise that cannot be written from them yet.

  Milestone 7 originally listed discard patterns among the failure classes to
  source a Mushling from, saying each "already has a real compiler
  diagnostic" and to use it verbatim. That was not true of this one, and the
  roadmap no longer claims it. Every form below compiles and the program
  exits zero:

    - a bare underscore as a match arm, which is the consequential one: a
      catch-all makes any match trivially exhaustive, so adding a variant to
      an enum stops forcing anyone to handle it.
    - a binding named with a bare underscore, discarding its value outright.
    - the underscore-prefixed form.

  MANIFESTO.md does not ban them either. The prohibition lives in AGENTS.md,
  which governs this compiler's own Rust source rather than the language it
  compiles; the two were conflated when the roadmap entry was written.

  So this file is not wired into `exercises()`. An exercise teaches a rule by
  showing its diagnostic, and there is no rule here yet to teach. Whether
  Cinnabar rejects discards is a language decision: it belongs in
  MANIFESTO.md first, and the exercise follows the rule rather than the other
  way round.

  `tests/language_questions.rs` asserts this compiles, so the record cannot
  rot unnoticed — it was broken for two commits before anything checked it.
|#

pub type Colour
  pub Red
  pub Green
end

pub fun classify(value: Colour) I64
  match value
    Red => return 1
    _ => return 0
  end
end

pub fun main() I64
  val _ = 5
  val _unused = 7
  if classify(Red) != 1
    return 1
  end
  if classify(Green) != 0
    return 2
  end
  return 0
end
